                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "As I write this, Tiles is on sale for 59 cents. I'd say it's worth around that much- I certainly wouldn't have been disappointed with what I got for that cost. At the normal cost of $4, however? Well, I beat the game in around 80 minutes. Spent 5 minutes in the level editor seeing how that worked (it's a good editor!) and then 15 blazing through 100 user levels for the last achievements. Yes, that means I attained 100% completion in less than 2 hours.\n\nIn terms of gameplay, you move a square around a grid of squares to step on all the blue squares without falling off, with the blue squares falling about 10 moves after you step on them when moving at full speed. Notice that \"at full speed\" part- that's crucial to the game. Tiles brands itself as a puzzle game, but other than plotting a rough circuit through the latter levels, it's pure twitchy action. You have to keep moving as everything is tied to real time. I'm a huge fan of Chip's Challenge, another puzzle game that has many real time mechanics, but in that game they work near seamlessly. Here? Here they just serve to frustrate more often than not.\n\nThere are 7 types of tile. Green, where you start, and can be stepped on infinitely. Red, where you end, and can only be stepped on when all tiles are set to fall. Blue, which fall and must be cleared. Frosted, which stand infinitely until stepped off of, at which point they become blue. Purple, which stay forever. Yellow, which start out a level set to fall. And lastly, orange, which act like purple, but appear and disappear.\n\nOrange tiles are stupid. You may recall that I emphasized at full speed earlier. That's because you often have to double back across a few already stepped tiles in order to keep progressing. Well, with the blue tiles set to fall, you can find yourself in a position where you have nowhere to go but the orange tile in front of you that's currently inactive and now you're dead because you didn't wait a tile beforehand to delay the last blue one falling so you could actually get to the next orange. Or maybe the orange is there and you step on it and it falls immediately and go do the entire level over again. It says a lot that I audibly groaned when they kept appearing in all parts of a level near the end of the game, and always tried to prioritize sections with them.\n\nLet's talk level design real quick. The designs were mostly uninteresting symmetric arrangements, and I'm not sure if the uninterestingness was due to weak design or weak mechanics. I feel like there's some potential here that went untapped, but despite the editor I'm not coming up with anything off the top of my head, and the small community for the most part didn't either- the 100 custom levels I played were often 5x5 simple paths. There was one that stuck out- a level shaped like a bomb that gave you about 15 seconds to complete it before the yellow tiles fell. That was awesome! Why wasn't there anything like that in the main game? It would have explained yellow tiles decently well, too!\n\nOh right, main game design. Pro tip: making the player do the same thing 4 times, once in each corner is bad design. If they can do it twice in a row, doing it 4 times is just tedium. Don't do that. Tiles does this for most levels, especially in the endgame. Those that lack fourfold symmetry still have twofold symmetry, which really doesn't help.\n\nIt's times like these I wish Steam had a \"meh\" review option.",
